# Chapter 8. Advanced Comptime Metaprogramming

Comptime in Zig is not merely a way to evaluate constants. It is a deliberate, integrated mechanism for running arbitrary Zig code during compilation, transforming type information, generating specialized functions, and building data structures that vanish at runtime. Where other languages resort to external code generators, macro systems, or template metaprogramming, Zig gives you a single, consistent language with full access to the compiler’s type introspection.

This chapter moves beyond simple comptime parameters and `comptime_int`. We’ll explore how to construct compile-time hash maps, generate whole API layers from struct definitions, implement embedded DSLs, and test comptime logic. You’ll also learn to avoid the pitfalls of runaway compile times and brittle generative code.

---

## 1. Basic Usage: The "How"

Advanced comptime metaprogramming revolves around three tools:

- **`comptime` blocks** – Code forced to run at compile time.
- **`@typeInfo`, `@TypeOf`, `@Type`** – Type introspection and construction.
- **Comptime parameters** – Functions that receive values known at compile time, enabling specialization.

### 1.1 Building a Compile-Time HashMap

Zig’s standard library provides `std.ComptimeStringMap`, but building your own compile-time associative structure illustrates the mechanics:

```zig
const std = @import("std");

fn ComptimeMap(comptime K: type, comptime V: type, comptime pairs: []const struct { K, V }) type {
    comptime var map: [pairs.len]struct { K, V } = pairs;
    // Sort at compile time to enable binary search
    comptime std.sort.block(pairs, struct {
        fn less(ctx: void, a: @TypeOf(pairs[0]), b: @TypeOf(pairs[0])) bool {
            _ = ctx;
            return a[0] < b[0];
        }
    }.less, {});

    return struct {
        pub fn get(key: K) ?V {
            const idx = comptime blk: {
                for (map, 0..) |pair, i| {
                    if (pair[0] == key) break :blk i;
                }
                break :blk null;
            };
            if (idx) |i| return map[i][1];
            return null;
        }
    };
}

// Usage: a compile-time lookup table for opcodes
const OpMap = ComptimeMap(u8, []const u8, .{
    .{ 0x01, "ADD" },
    .{ 0x02, "SUB" },
    .{ 0x03, "MUL" },
});

test "compile-time map" {
    try std.testing.expectEqualStrings("ADD", OpMap.get(0x01).?);
    try std.testing.expect(OpMap.get(0xFF) == null);
}
```

### 1.2 Generating API Layers from Type Info

Given a struct definition, you can generate serialization, validation, or accessor functions:

```zig
const std = @import("std");

fn makeGetters(comptime T: type) type {
    const fields = std.meta.fields(T);
    var methods: []const std.builtin.Type.Declaration = &[_]std.builtin.Type.Declaration{};

    inline for (fields) |field| {
        const name = comptime "get_" ++ field.name;
        const decl: std.builtin.Type.Declaration = .{
            .name = name,
            .alignment = 0,
            .init = struct {
                fn getter(instance: *const T) @TypeOf(@field(instance, field.name)) {
                    return @field(instance, field.name);
                }
            }.getter,
        };
        methods = methods ++ &[_]std.builtin.Type.Declaration{decl};
    }

    return @Type(.{
        .Struct = .{
            .layout = .Auto,
            .fields = &[_]std.builtin.Type.StructField{},
            .decls = methods,
            .is_tuple = false,
        },
    });
}

const Point = struct { x: i32, y: i32 };
const Getters = makeGetters(Point);

test "generated getters" {
    var p = Point{ .x = 42, .y = 100 };
    try std.testing.expect(Getters.get_x(&p) == 42);
    try std.testing.expect(Getters.get_y(&p) == 100);
}
```

### 1.3 Compile-Time DSL for State Machines

Define a state machine description at compile time and generate transitions:

```zig
const std = @import("std");

fn StateMachine(comptime States: type, comptime Transitions: anytype) type {
    const state_fields = std.meta.fields(States);
    return struct {
        state: States,

        pub fn transition(self: *@This(), comptime event: []const u8) !void {
            inline for (Transitions) |t| {
                if (t.from == self.state and std.mem.eql(u8, t.event, event)) {
                    self.state = t.to;
                    return;
                }
            }
            return error.InvalidTransition;
        }
    };
}

const MyStates = enum { Idle, Active, Error };
const transitions = .{
    .{ .from = MyStates.Idle, .event = "start", .to = MyStates.Active },
    .{ .from = MyStates.Active, .event = "fail", .to = MyStates.Error },
    .{ .from = MyStates.Error, .event = "reset", .to = MyStates.Idle },
};

const MyMachine = StateMachine(MyStates, transitions);

test "state machine" {
    var machine = MyMachine{ .state = MyStates.Idle };
    try machine.transition("start");
    try std.testing.expect(machine.state == MyStates.Active);
    try machine.transition("fail");
    try std.testing.expect(machine.state == MyStates.Error);
    try machine.transition("reset");
    try std.testing.expect(machine.state == MyStates.Idle);
}
```

