## 7. Best Practices: The "Idiomatic Way"

### 7.1 Prefer `comptime` Parameters Over `@TypeOf` Dispatch

When you need compile-time polymorphism, accept a `comptime` parameter rather than inspecting a runtime value’s type with `@TypeOf`. The former makes the specialization explicit and is less error-prone.

```zig
// Good: explicit comptime parameter
fn serialize(comptime T: type, value: T) void { ... }

// Avoid: runtime value with type inspection at comptime
fn serialize(value: anytype) void {
    const T = @TypeOf(value);
    // ... same body as above, but the call site doesn't show specialization
}
```

The `anytype` pattern is sometimes idiomatic (e.g., `std.json.stringify`), but it hides the fact that the function is generic. Use it sparingly and document clearly.

### 7.2 Keep Comptime-Only Code Behind Guards

If a block of code is only valid at comptime (e.g., it accesses fields of a type that may not exist at runtime), guard it with `if (comptime) ...` or place it inside a `comptime` block. This prevents accidental runtime execution.

```zig
const has_field = comptime std.meta.hasField(T, "extra");
if (has_field) {
    // This block's condition is comptime-known, so the block may still be
    // type-checked. Ensure it would type-check even if has_field is false.
}
```

### 7.3 Use `inline` Loops Only When Needed

An `inline for` duplicates the loop body for each iteration and can dramatically increase compile time. Only use it when you genuinely need to unroll the loop at compile time (e.g., to generate a switch case for each enum variant). Otherwise, use a regular `for` loop even inside comptime code – regular loops are executed by the interpreter without unrolling.

```zig
// Good for comptime iteration over small, known collections
comptime var sum = 0;
for (0..10) |i| {
    sum += i;  // Not inline, just normal loop
}

// Use inline only when you need unrolled code generation
inline for (std.meta.fields(MyEnum)) |field| {
    // Generate a case for each enum field
}
```

### 7.4 Cache Expensive Comptime Computations

If you have a heavy comptime function, assign its result to a `comptime` constant and reuse it across multiple generic instantiations.

```zig
const PrecomputedTable = comptime buildTable(1024);  // built once

fn generic(comptime T: type) void {
    _ = PrecomputedTable.get(T);  // no recomputation
}
```

### 7.5 Test Comptime Logic with Comptime Tests

Use the `test` block with comptime assertions to validate your metaprograms:

```zig
test "comptime map works" {
    const map = ComptimeMap(u8, u8, .{.{1, 2}});
    comptime {
        std.testing.expect(map.get(1).? == 2);
    }
}
```

This ensures your compile-time data structures are correct before they are used in runtime code.

